abstract: >-
  Machine-readable crosswalks between four pairs of industrial data standards,
  together with the measurement that explains why such crosswalks fail. Each
  standard is scored by an Axiomatic Strength Index, the count of its
  contradiction-capable axioms. Standards whose index is zero are shown to be
  refutation-inert: no set of class correspondences can make any of their classes
  unsatisfiable, so a clean reasoner report against them is not evidence of a good
  alignment. Across the pairs studied the index differs by up to three orders of
  magnitude, which places all of the detectable logical risk on one side of each
  crosswalk. The flagship IDO to IFC4 crosswalk is promoted to OWL and certified
  with the HermiT reasoner, and demonstrates that every correspondence can pass
  validation individually while the set collapses when asserted together.
